INT. NORTHSIDE RECORDING STUDIO - NIGHT

A small vocal booth. Foam on the walls. A red light is on.

TIM, 28, boxer, stands at the microphone. He wears a hooded training jacket. His hands are wrapped. He holds a paper with lyrics, but he does not look at it.

Behind the glass, his trainer TOM, 55, sits next to a young ENGINEER with big headphones. A sleepy bulldog lies under the mixing desk.

ENGINEER
(through speaker)
Okay. Take four.

Tim nods. Music starts, a heavy beat. He opens his mouth. Nothing comes.

The music stops.

TOM
(through speaker)
It's okay. Again.

TIM
Again.

The beat starts. Tim says two words, then stops. He rubs his wrapped knuckles.

TOM
(through speaker)
You have the words?

TIM
I have the words.

TOM
Then say them.

Tim looks at the paper. Folds it. Puts it in his pocket.

TIM
Is not a song. It is only noise.

TOM
Tomorrow forty thousand people hear this noise when you walk to the ring.

TIM
Yes. I know.

The Engineer looks at his screen. Very busy suddenly.

Tom takes off his headphones and goes to the booth. He opens the door. The bulldog lifts its head, then lays it down again.

Tom stands close. He fixes Tim's collar. His hands are not quite steady.

TOM
Your father had the same face. Before his last fight.

TIM
You said he was not afraid.

TOM
I said many things.

Tim looks at him a long time. Then he laughs once, short.

TIM
You are a bad liar, Tom.

TOM
I am a good trainer.

TIM
Same thing, in your case.

Tom tries to smile. It doesn't hold. He goes back to the control room. Sits. Puts on the headphones.

TOM (CONT'D)
(through speaker)
From the top. Do not think. Just talk.

The beat. Tim closes his eyes.

TIM
(quietly, to the beat)
He is waiting at the door. He is waiting for me.

Tom looks down at his own hands.

TIM (CONT'D)
(a little louder)
Let him wait.

The Engineer stops pretending to be busy. He is looking at Tim now.

The red light stays on.
